-- title:
--   Lemma 48 — reweighted decomposition A = B + Q + P with P ⩾ 0, Q centered, bounded, variance ⩽ Π[a,b]
-- statement:
--   Let the outside relations be sampled independently from $D_{ij}$, and let $\Pi$ have entries in $[0,1]$ and $\rho_{ij}[a,b]\ge\Pi[a,b]$. For the matrices in Definition 47,
--   $$A=B+Q+P,\quad P\ge0,\quad \mathbb E Q=0,\quad |Q_{ia,jb}|\le1,\quad \operatorname{Var}(Q_{ia,jb})\le\Pi[a,b]\quad(i\ne j).$$
--   The decomposition holds for every possible sample (each pair's label is in its distribution's support). Nonnegativity and the absolute bound hold even on other label tables; the expectation and variance are taken under the outside product law. No conclusion is assumed as a field of the model.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: finite variables and alphabet → variables and alphabet indexed by Fin n and Fin q; unordered pairs represented by i < j; reason: finite relabelling is bijective and counts every pair once
--   - generalization: labels y of a fixed CSP type, with symmetric relations R_y → all symmetric Boolean relations are available as labels; reason: pushforward along y ↦ R_y preserves every constraint and violation probability; each original type is a subclass
--   - encoding: independent outside labels distributed as D_ij → product measure of the finite relation-valued PMFs on unordered outside pairs; reason: the joint law has full independence, not just pairwise independence; all events and moments are measurable
--   - generalization: Π from the FFK certifiable model → any Π in [0,1] dominated by the violation probabilities; reason: neither symmetry, PSD dominance nor the positive-entry lower bound is needed for these identities; the model is included
--   - encoding: every graph generated by the model → decomposition for every table with each sampled label in its pair distribution support; reason: finite sampling never produces a zero-mass label; P and the absolute bound remain quantified over every table
-- source:
--   Kothari, Louis, Paul and Raghavendra, Recovering Planted Subgraphs in Smoothed Models, November 3, 2026, Lemma 48, printed p. 31 (PDF p. 33), PDF SHA256 0f9dc5d96e197a1868bf10cade60fcb647db289ebc51985e4d167c942c54f82b

import Definitions.Def_SmoothedPlanted_FFK_Reweighted

open scoped BigOperators
open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

noncomputable section

namespace SmoothedPlanted.FFK

theorem lemma_48 {m q : ℕ} (D : Pair (Fin m) → PMF (Relation q))
    (Pi : Matrix (Fin q) (Fin q) ℝ)
    (hbox : ∀ a b, 0 ≤ Pi a b ∧ Pi a b ≤ 1) (hD : Dominates D Pi) :
    (∀ Y : CSP (Fin m) q, (∀ e, Y e ∈ (D e).support) →
      adjacency (incompatibilityPrime Y) = biasedMatrix Pi + reweightedQ D Pi Y + reweightedP D Pi Y) ∧
    (∀ (Y : CSP (Fin m) q) x y, 0 ≤ reweightedP D Pi Y x y) ∧
    (∀ x y : Fin m × Fin q, ∫ Y, reweightedQ D Pi Y x y ∂outsideLaw D = 0) ∧
    (∀ (Y : CSP (Fin m) q) (i j : Fin m) (a b : Fin q), i ≠ j →
      |reweightedQ D Pi Y (i,a) (j,b)| ≤ 1) ∧
    (∀ (i j : Fin m) (a b : Fin q), i ≠ j →
      variance (fun Y => reweightedQ D Pi Y (i,a) (j,b)) (outsideLaw D) ≤ Pi a b) := by sorry

end SmoothedPlanted.FFK
